The Python memory provider became a Nodecode cell. Kept as the original
writes them: the two exchanges (a client_credentials token from the origin's
/token, then one JSON-RPC tools/call of GBrain's search), the arguments that
search carries (the first 500 characters of the operator's message, the
limit, snippet_chars 1, fields lean, the optional source id), the envelope
reading (plain JSON or SSE data: lines), the row rules (a row GBrain flags
injection_suspected is dropped before duplicates are counted, a slug, a
title and a source id and nothing else survive), the pointer line and the
header above it, the one-turn-later hand-over, the bounded bodies, and the
fail-open posture. The daemon thread is an NLK:WORKER-START, and the
injection seam is the harness's own: the request is written through
NLK:RECORD-TURN-CONTEXT-PROMPT at the :ROUND point.

Left out, because Hermes' shape is not Nodecode's: the memory-provider
interface itself (initialize, sync_turn, queue_prefetch and prefetch, recall
status, the reset and rewind generations, shutdown) -- a Nodecode cell reads
a turn's input from the store and writes into the request it is building, so
there is no provider object to keep in step; the per-profile secret scope,
the credential-fingerprint cache and the in-flight bookkeeping, which exist
so one Hermes process can serve several routed profiles, where a Nodecode
home is a process; the custom opener that ignores proxies and refuses
redirects, which is the harness's HTTP client now; and Hermes'
is_trivial_prompt, replaced by a local test of a slash command, a greeting,
an acknowledgement, or fewer than three words.

Added, because a Nodecode cell can carry them: the gbrain_pointer tool,
/gbrain-pointer and a manual topic, so a search can be asked for now
instead of waiting for the next turn.
